#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554705} :activity/id #uuid "80dffb9b-6686-5250-93b4-d26b471b47b4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "092ff8d8-cf90-56ef-8c25-e605f251193c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474494} {:answer/id #uuid "f435ae19-d966-5bc7-9269-fe24671a9719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474494} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa760edb-c42e-5286-8483-d57d5dc10238" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475173} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a15a1e81-02c1-5e18-b7bc-07b26f9384fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474496} {:answer/id #uuid "2a16a913-6add-5730-88f1-0650356168a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474497} :answer-field/id #uuid "54555e33-802a-5a49-92b9-9f62666a56e5" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "457c64dc-56fb-5c01-a526-273fb7c1a2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474498} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb666e3-c28f-57c2-b34a-73ed59f5b4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474498} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ab95613-7503-59aa-9a5e-45419ccd0135" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0812b1fb-a07e-5022-afd7-9a772e5b6236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474500} {:answer/id #uuid "af0f1167-9279-5382-98c3-8216e3f19902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474501} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc057c0-060c-5d7a-a86f-85031f906e13" :answer-field/key "D" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f6e52f3-8502-516e-a6ab-c65771746796" :question/math-academy-id "q-164168" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f3852805b0ae44433828113396fa63d0688ec06ae57886428815515e521baa49.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $B$ and $D.$ Hence, $B$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D({x}_{D},{y}_{D},{z}_{D}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{D},{y}_{D})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](images/f1/f10742ca92d2429c35b36cf77ae2d857774faba848a74291bbfa8e5ac71eeada.png)"} :step/id #uuid "0e56b147-81e4-5b76-ba78-f42888fc798d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9a8d6e4-83ad-5779-b6b1-8d3f2f9ddd0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474478} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3a7e62-eb4d-5b61-9aa1-0d372465232c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474479} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ef37d28-32ff-5d41-97ce-27cbd12aaa3d" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475165} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47c7161a-6281-5453-ba04-08a2ff9730cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474480} {:answer/id #uuid "adc00843-8143-55a5-b0fe-475c44f606db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474480} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f0324d3-98fc-5c1c-a873-f07d8e16c52d" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ff8f7eb-17b6-54d7-ade8-e5cacfad8918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474482} {:answer/id #uuid "3f27ba75-5695-5a59-96ba-eacafc25acb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474483} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7524397-46db-58f5-a737-2282ef746679" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47992fdc-ac9e-5b34-9ffc-1e1720081dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474484} {:answer/id #uuid "e264ae91-5911-505d-a745-60ea3f527160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474484} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bce15c3-2af0-5965-946b-f94b71b680d6" :answer-field/key "D" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e433d738-c2a7-5a16-852b-741bb762e1fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-164101" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4ca398cfb365346962fc66ffecd7fb85390a9a98c49cb1926f6d8093a01c8bc.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence, $A$ and $C$ are local minima.\n\n![](images/02/0262a775dad79697ea888b6047e607c12ec804dbb734b72b2ca8fda54d6c130f.png)"} :step/id #uuid "7aed301f-ecb7-5892-aaed-84080d4f66c0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554705 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002ed759-21f8-5564-aa23-e5ae38d108b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434656} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf6cd21-6e6c-5f23-90a9-561d369a6b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434687} {:answer/id #uuid "ada293be-8d8f-53b2-8e22-8254f87db838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434708} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ef5c12-e171-59db-9f19-75d60d6fc0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434729} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc3a5a8-400b-5371-8e6e-5ad2e09e9946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434733}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434656} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9aa5f48-03a4-5e22-bfab-3a430f54be65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ae219904-d456-5249-bf4b-05599f3e75b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-163774" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1+\\sqrt{26}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1-\\sqrt{26},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both negative and positive eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1+\\sqrt{26}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-1-\\sqrt{26}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "90845892-a21e-5ed0-8f4d-4176220e88dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf8f149-6cad-5a5c-9d26-11b86bcaa720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465519} {:answer/id #uuid "6b04f584-4740-59cf-8497-f747191febb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465520} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddfefddd-b168-5878-87f2-f8f0e1876a2e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466603} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9329ab-4f40-5e23-86a5-bf4d0b285377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465521} {:answer/id #uuid "5441ae78-27c1-5694-8618-d6f72b5ef496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465522} :answer-field/id #uuid "d143db0c-e1c0-5daa-9470-d0193838ae88" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12620dec-4540-5bfc-a97c-4be11c2d223d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465523} {:answer/id #uuid "13ec6254-73f7-5076-8506-15526b0e95bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465523} :answer-field/id #uuid "c993b7a5-1549-5886-b715-49e5a62df038" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "00b2158a-941e-5a2f-b68c-c230bee6df19" :question/math-academy-id "q-163791" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈8+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $det(A)=-19$\n- **II.** $A$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a local minimum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(11)-1(-3) \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}|=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ab83c715-96e6-5979-ad33-c4a45c733837" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e792ff8-af62-57d5-a566-a450e4d2c073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465513} {:answer/id #uuid "c06e501a-7fca-57b5-8fd8-00c7ba0828ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465514}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465513} :answer-field/id #uuid "7767a756-a8d3-5be5-bfc9-c38d9f8739ab" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d91f22ee-b903-5aa9-a3a0-e9971360e020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465515} {:answer/id #uuid "ddaa9832-b748-5975-984c-dd66a942f7ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465516} :answer-field/id #uuid "caa63e50-d620-5761-8485-c5faf4821dd9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466601} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "476a495d-1c2e-59e7-a3f1-54bf4179f4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465517} {:answer/id #uuid "6a781903-27e5-5a45-afd1-4d6f7a5fdce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465518}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465518} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b937cb6-cf67-521a-8508-427cc88aed42" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c598e2d2-d26a-59ea-a0e5-a7e53e350ac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163789" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈16+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=3$ and $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a saddle point of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=3,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3)-1(-2)-4(7) \\\\  & =-17<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=3>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=5>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-17<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "e73ff290-95f1-54f7-9f82-02df0d72039f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554721}}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553849}]}
